---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on router16, the transit router that bridges Campus 1 (mswitch0)
to the backbone (router7). Its OSPF daemon (ospfd) is not running and its FRR
running configuration contains no OSPF block whatsoever — only the bare FRR
defaults. As a result, router16 holds only its two directly-connected routes
(10.2.2.0/24 on bond0 toward mswitch0, and 172.30.1.0/24 on eth2 toward
router7) and participates in no OSPF adjacency in either direction.

The downstream effect is two-fold. First, mswitch0 forms no OSPF neighbour
(its neighbour table is empty) so, once any stale LSAs age out, it loses all
learned routes and returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" for every off-
campus destination — exactly what pc5 observes when pinging 10.0.0.1. Second,
router7 and the rest of the backbone never receive LSAs advertising
172.16.10/20/30, so they have no return path to Campus 1 even if a packet
somehow arrived.

The team's suspicion that mswitch0 is at fault is incorrect: mswitch0's ospfd
is running, its network statements cover all four relevant prefixes
(10.2.2.0/24, 172.16.10/20/30/24), and it is simply waiting for a neighbour
that never comes. The actual broken component is router16's ospfd process on
its bond0 (10.2.2.1) and eth2 (172.30.1.1) interfaces.

Root cause: router16's ospfd is completely absent — neither running nor
configured.

Corrective fix: Add an OSPF process to router16's frr.conf (or via vtysh):
  router ospf
    network 10.2.2.0/24 area 0
    network 172.30.1.0/24 area 0
then restart ospfd on router16. This will bring up adjacencies with both
mswitch0 and router7, redistribute the Campus 1 VLAN prefixes into the
backbone OSPF domain, and restore full reachability for PC5–PC14.
=== END ===
```